1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,364
Every day, millions of people ask a voice assistant about the weather.

2
00:00:04,464 --> 00:00:06,851
But what they actually want to know is:

3
00:00:06,951 --> 00:00:08,510
should I go out, and when?

4
00:00:08,610 --> 00:00:10,296
Outing Copilot answers that.

5
00:00:10,696 --> 00:00:12,461
It's a self-hosted MCP server.

6
00:00:12,561 --> 00:00:16,360
For each question it chains five free public services:

7
00:00:16,460 --> 00:00:22,424
location, hourly forecast, air quality, public holidays and official weather alerts.

8
00:00:22,524 --> 00:00:25,023
And it turns them into one decision:

9
00:00:25,123 --> 00:00:29,644
the best window, a go, maybe or skip verdict, and what to bring.

10
00:00:30,044 --> 00:00:36,007
It runs over Streamable HTTP on the official MCP Python SDK.

11
00:00:36,107 --> 00:00:40,568
Here it negotiates spec version 2025-11-25.

12
00:00:40,968 --> 00:00:43,074
This is a simulated Alexa+ session.

13
00:00:43,174 --> 00:00:48,443
A real MCP client connects to the server, and the household starts talking.

14
00:00:48,843 --> 00:00:50,820
First, it learns where home is.

15
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:54,040
That's saved, so it still knows next week.

16
00:00:54,440 --> 00:00:56,676
When's the best time to walk the dog?

17
00:00:56,776 --> 00:01:04,157
It only looks at hours that start after sunrise and end by sunset, and scores each one for that activity.

18
00:01:04,257 --> 00:01:06,864
Dogs don't mind a drizzle, picnics do.

19
00:01:06,964 --> 00:01:07,648
The answer:

20
00:01:07,748 --> 00:01:11,852
8 to 9 AM, in one sentence, plus a card for screen devices.

21
00:01:12,252 --> 00:01:17,597
Tell it someone in the family has asthma, and it uses a stricter air-quality limit from then on.

22
00:01:17,697 --> 00:01:24,120
Ask about a two-hour picnic this weekend, and it compares Saturday and Sunday, and warns you it'll be chilly.

23
00:01:24,520 --> 00:01:25,109
Or plan ahead:

24
00:01:25,209 --> 00:01:29,280
it ranks the whole week for a run, and Sunday wins with 98.

25
00:01:29,380 --> 00:01:31,330
It's upfront about gaps, too:

26
00:01:31,430 --> 00:01:32,037
"Heads up:

27
00:01:32,137 --> 00:01:39,460
air-quality data isn't available that far out, so I've docked those days." It knows what it doesn't know.

28
00:01:39,860 --> 00:01:41,408
Some city names are ambiguous.

29
00:01:41,508 --> 00:01:51,608
Ask about Portland, and it tells you which one it picked, Portland, Oregon, with a 10 to 11 AM walk tomorrow, and how to switch if you meant Portland, Maine.

30
00:01:52,008 --> 00:02:03,660
All 47 tests pass, offline, against recorded API responses, including weather alerts, missing air-quality data, ambiguous places and upstream errors.

31
00:02:04,059 --> 00:02:04,841
Outing Copilot:

32
00:02:04,941 --> 00:02:06,888
from forecast to decision.

33
00:02:06,988 --> 00:02:10,825
The code is open source, and it needs no API keys.

34
00:02:10,925 --> 00:02:12,400
Thanks for watching.
